Much of the answer lies in food formulation.

8
00:01:00,924 --> 00:01:13,493
Emulsifiers and stabilisers perform different but complementary functions, helping products retain the texture, structure and stability consumers expect from production through distribution and consumption.

9
00:01:13,493 --> 00:01:22,959
Emulsifiers: Bringing Unlikely Ingredients Together An emulsion is a mixture of two liquids that do not naturally mix, typically oil and water.

10
00:01:22,959 --> 00:01:26,118
Left alone, the two phases separate.

11
00:01:26,118 --> 00:01:34,061
Emulsifiers help overcome this problem because their molecular structure contains both water-attracting and oil-attracting portions.

12
00:01:34,061 --> 00:01:42,777
By positioning themselves at the interface between oil and water, they reduce surface tension and help one phase remain dispersed within the other.

13
00:01:42,777 --> 00:01:51,754
Common emulsifiers include lecithin, mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids, polysorbates, sodium stearoyl lactylate and DATEM.

14
00:01:51,754 --> 00:01:56,879
Certain proteins and gum arabic can also perform emulsifying functions.

15
00:01:56,879 --> 00:02:00,845
Their applications are visible across everyday foods.

16
00:02:00,845 --> 00:02:05,720
In salad dressings and mayonnaise, emulsifiers prevent oil separation.

17
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:09,993
In margarine and spreads, they keep fat evenly distributed.

18
00:02:09,993 --> 00:02:19,538
In baked products, they help incorporate fat and can strengthen gluten networks, improve gas retention, increase volume and contribute to softness.

19
00:02:19,538 --> 00:02:23,402
Ice cream provides a particularly familiar example.

20
00:02:23,402 --> 00:02:31,788
Emulsifiers help control fat globule size and promote partial coalescence, contributing to smooth texture and resistance to melting.

21
00:02:31,788 --> 00:02:39,811
In chocolate, lecithin can reduce viscosity, improving flow during moulding and coating while helping maintain a glossy finish.

